Characteristics are the reusable building blocks that OSW uses to describe the ''values'' an entry can carry. This article explains what a characteristic is, how a characteristic type generates its own schema, and how characteristics are reused across categories.

{{#if: {{#ask: [[:Category:OSW4082937906634af992cf9a1b18d772cf]] |?HasName= |mainlabel=- }} || <div style="border-left:4px solid #c8ccd1; padding:0.5em 1em; margin-bottom:1em;">'''Note:''' the quantitative characteristics package is not installed on this wiki, so the quantity pages used as examples below do not exist here and those links appear red. Everything else on this page applies unchanged.</div>}}

= Why characteristics exist =

A plain JSON Schema property describes a value with a type, for example <code>"type": "number"</code>. That is enough to validate the value, but it says nothing about what the number ''means'', in which unit it is given, or which semantic property it should be stored under.

A characteristic bundles all of that into one reusable object:

* the structure of the value (which fields it consists of)
* its meaning (which semantic property it maps to)
* its presentation (label and description, in several languages)
* for quantities, the unit and the allowed alternative units

Once defined, a characteristic can be used as a field in any number of categories, in the same way a class is reused in a programming language. Changing the characteristic changes every category that uses it.

= Three levels =

Characteristics live on three levels. Keeping them apart is the key to understanding the whole system.

{| class="wikitable"
!Level
!Page
!Role
|-
|Metacategory
|[[:Category:OSWffe74f291d354037b318c422591c5023|Characteristic Type]]
|Holds the <code>schema_template</code>. It knows ''how to build'' the schema of a characteristic out of a description of its properties.
|-
|Characteristic
|e. g. [[:Category:OSW93ccae36243542ceac6c951450a81d47|Characteristic]] and its subcategories
|A concrete building block, for example [[:Category:OSW4082937906634af992cf9a1b18d772cf|Quantity Value]] or a length derived from it. Its <code>jsonschema</code> slot is generated by the metacategory.
|-
|Value
|inside the jsondata of an entry
|The actual value, for example <code>{"value": 30, "unit": "Item:OSWf101d25e944856e3bd4b4c9863db7de2"}</code>.
|}

The important consequence: '''you do not write the jsonschema of a characteristic by hand'''. You describe its properties in the jsondata slot, and the metacategory generates the schema when the page is saved.

= Defining a characteristic =

A characteristic page is a page in the <code>Category</code> namespace whose <code>type</code> is a characteristic type. In its jsondata you list the properties the characteristic consists of, under <code>properties</code>. Each entry describes one field.

You normally do not type this JSON by hand. Because the page declares a characteristic type as its <code>type</code>, the editor builds a form from the <code>jsonschema</code> of that type, for example [[:Category:OSWffe74f291d354037b318c422591c5023|Characteristic Type]], and that form edits the <code>jsondata</code> slot of your page. So the schema of the metacategory describes ''what you may fill in'', and your answers land in jsondata. The examples below show that jsondata, which is what the form produces.

The characteristic every quantity is built from, [[:Category:OSW4082937906634af992cf9a1b18d772cf|Quantity Value]], consists of exactly two such entries, a number and the unit it is given in:

<syntaxhighlight lang="json">
{
    "uuid": "ed470b63-e754-41bc-813a-b3aa0087a847",
    "name": "value",
    "label": [
        {"text": "Value", "lang": "en"},
        {"text": "Wert", "lang": "de"}
    ],
    "required": true,
    "type": "number"
}
</syntaxhighlight>

<syntaxhighlight lang="json">
{
    "uuid": "fc310fe8-f963-4841-9fff-126a15a41556",
    "name": "unit",
    "label": [
        {"text": "Unit", "lang": "en"},
        {"text": "Einheit", "lang": "de"}
    ],
    "type": "string",
    "recommended": true
}
</syntaxhighlight>

Two details are worth noting in this real example. Each entry carries its own <code>uuid</code>, which stays stable when the property is renamed or relabelled. And besides <code>required</code> there is <code>recommended</code>, which does not enforce a value but marks the field as one the editor should offer prominently.

== From jsondata to generated schema ==

On save, the <code>schema_template</code> of the metacategory turns every such entry into a JSON Schema property and writes the result into the <code>jsonschema</code> slot of the page. The two jsondata entries above become this schema (shortened):

<syntaxhighlight lang="json">
{
    "allOf": [
        {"$ref": "#/$defs/generated"}
    ],
    "format": "grid-strict",
    "properties": {
        "value": {"options": {"grid_columns": 4}},
        "unit": {"options": {"grid_columns": 4}}
    },
    "title": "QuantityValue",
    "$defs": {
        "generated": {
            "$comment": "Autogenerated section - do not edit. Generated from Category:Category Category:OSWffe74f291d354037b318c422591c5023",
            "allOf": [
                {"$ref": "/wiki/Category:OSW93ccae36243542ceac6c951450a81d47?action=raw&slot=jsonschema"}
            ],
            "type": "object",
            "title": "GeneratedQuantityValue",
            "required": ["type", "value"],
            "properties": {
                "type": {"default": ["Category:OSW4082937906634af992cf9a1b18d772cf"]},
                "value": {"title": "value", "title*": {"en": "Value", "de": "Wert"}, "type": "number"},
                "unit": {"title": "unit", "title*": {"en": "Unit", "de": "Einheit"}, "type": "string"}
            }
        }
    }
}
</syntaxhighlight>

Every field you filled in has a counterpart here: <code>name</code> became the property key, <code>label</code> became <code>title*</code> per language, <code>type</code> became the JSON Schema type, and <code>required</code> became the <code>required</code> list. The <code>allOf</code> inside the generated section is the inheritance from the parent characteristic.

=== What $defs separates ===

The schema has two halves, and the split is what makes the page editable at all:

* '''everything inside <code>$defs.generated</code>''' is written by the template on every save. It carries the <code>$comment</code> "Autogenerated section - do not edit". Any change you make there is lost the next time the page is saved.
* '''everything outside <code>$defs.generated</code>''' is yours and is preserved. The generated part is pulled in through <code>"allOf": [{"$ref": "#/$defs/generated"}]</code>, so your half inherits all generated properties and can add to them.

In the example above the manual half is small but real: <code>"format": "grid-strict"</code> and the <code>grid_columns</code> options arrange value and unit next to each other in the editor. Neither can be expressed in the jsondata property list, so they are written into the schema by hand and survive every regeneration.

This is the usual pattern for manual additions: presentation options, an extra field the template cannot express, a stricter constraint on a generated property. Refine a generated property by naming it again in the outer <code>properties</code>. Because <code>allOf</code> merges both halves, the outer entry adds to the generated one rather than replacing it.

== Type of a property ==

The <code>type</code> field decides how the property is rendered and stored. It is the discriminator of the whole system, and exactly one of the following:

{| class="wikitable"
!<code>type</code>
!Meaning
!Stored as
|-
|<code>number</code>, <code>integer</code>
|a numeric value
|number
|-
|<code>string</code>
|a text value
|text
|-
|<code>boolean</code>
|yes or no
|boolean
|-
|<code>iri</code>
|a reference to another entry, edited with autocomplete. Combine with <code>range</code> to restrict which category may be selected.
|page reference
|-
|<code>object</code>
|a nested characteristic. Combine with <code>characteristic</code> to name the characteristic to nest.
|subobject
|}

An older field named <code>property_type</code> with values such as <code>SimpleProperty</code> is deprecated and must not be used in new characteristics.

== Naming a property ==

The <code>name</code> becomes the key in the jsondata and therefore part of the stored data. It has to be lowercase, without spaces, using underscores as separator, for example <code>street</code>, <code>postal_code</code>, <code>temp_i_avg</code>. Renaming it later changes the shape of all existing data, so it is worth choosing carefully. The human readable form belongs in <code>label</code>, not in <code>name</code>.

= Using a characteristic in a category =

A characteristic becomes a field of a category by referencing its schema. In the <code>jsondata</code> of the consuming category you name the characteristic; in the generated <code>jsonschema</code> this becomes a <code>$ref</code>:

<syntaxhighlight lang="json">
{
    "properties": {
        "physical_dimension": {
            "title": "Physical dimension",
            "$ref": "/wiki/Category:OSW39fd0aff7afa470c9725b715929d6982?action=raw&slot=jsonschema"
        }
    }
}
</syntaxhighlight>

When writing a schema by hand, always use <code>$ref</code>. The form <code>"type": "object", "characteristic": "Category:..."</code> belongs in the jsondata slot only, where it is the input the template converts into the <code>$ref</code> above.

Because a characteristic is referenced rather than copied, the same building block can appear many times, in the same category or in different ones. A category may for example have both a <code>minimal_physical_dimension</code> and a <code>maximal_physical_dimension</code>, both referencing the same characteristic.

= Older JsonSchema pages =

You will still meet an earlier mechanism for reusing a structure: a schema fragment stored in the <code>JsonSchema</code> namespace, for example [[JsonSchema:PostalAddress]], which defines the fields <code>street_name</code>, <code>house_no</code>, <code>postal_code</code>, <code>city</code> and so on. It is referenced in the same way, with a <code>$ref</code>:

<syntaxhighlight lang="json">
{
    "properties": {
        "postal_address": {
            "$ref": "/wiki/JsonSchema:PostalAddress?action=raw"
        }
    }
}
</syntaxhighlight>

'''These pages are being replaced by characteristics.''' Use a characteristic for anything new, and expect the existing <code>JsonSchema</code> pages to be migrated over time.

The reason for the move is that a <code>JsonSchema</code> page is only a fragment of schema text. It is not an entry, so it cannot be queried, annotated, subclassed or referred to by other data. A characteristic is a real page in the <code>Category</code> namespace, so all of that becomes possible, and its schema is generated rather than maintained by hand.

= How characteristic values are stored =

A characteristic value is an object, so it cannot be stored as a single semantic property. It is stored as a '''subobject''' of the page, and the page keeps a reference to it.

Each subobject gets an identifier. If the value carries a <code>uuid</code>, that is used. Otherwise the identifier is derived from the position of the value in the jsondata, which is what makes several slots of the same type distinguishable:

<pre>
Item:OSW...#physical_dimension
Item:OSW...#physical_dimension.l
Item:OSW...#physical_dimension.d
</pre>

The parent additionally links to each characteristic slot under a property named after the field, for example <code>HasCharacteristic_physical_dimension</code>, in addition to the general <code>HasCharacteristic</code>. This means a query can address one specific slot rather than all values of the same type at once.

Each subobject carries its field name as display title, so the slots can also be told apart when following the general property:

<syntaxhighlight lang="text">
{{#ask: [[Category:MyCategory]]
 |?HasCharacteristic.Display title of
 |?HasCharacteristic.HasLengthValue
}}
</syntaxhighlight>

= Quantities are characteristics too =

A quantity such as a length or a temperature is a characteristic with a fixed structure: a numeric <code>value</code> and a <code>unit</code>. These are built from [[:Category:OSW4082937906634af992cf9a1b18d772cf|Quantity Value]] and typed by the metacategory [[:Category:OSWac07a46c2cf14f3daec503136861f5ab|Quantity Value Type]].

What a quantity characteristic adds on top of an ordinary one:

* a '''quantity kind''', stating what is being measured
* a '''default unit''', preselected in the editor
* a '''unit enumeration''', the alternative units offered in the editor
* a '''quantity property''', the semantic property the value is stored under, for example <code>HasLengthValue</code>

Because the unit is part of the value, the stored quantity keeps its unit, and a query can compare values that were entered in different units.

Creating a new quantity, or adding a unit to an existing one, is described in [[Item:OSWe03dc32cf6b747e4b49c5fe54db3ba1d|Quantities and units]].

= Summary =

* A characteristic is a reusable, typed building block for values.
* You edit its jsondata through a form built from the schema of its characteristic type.
* Its schema is '''generated''' from that jsondata into <code>$defs.generated</code>. Do not edit that part by hand.
* Everything outside <code>$defs.generated</code> is preserved, so presentation options and extra constraints can be added there.
* <code>type</code> selects one of number, integer, string, boolean, iri or object.
* Categories reuse a characteristic with <code>$ref</code>, as often as they like.
* Values are stored as subobjects that are individually addressable, so several slots of the same type stay distinguishable.